          deepDive: "**V-る + べからず** is a classical prohibition, *do not ~, ~ is forbidden*. It is the negative of the classical auxiliary べし (*should, must*): べから + ず (classical *not*). The tone is stern, impersonal and old-fashioned, which is why it survives on **signs, notices, rules and maxims**: 立ち入るべからず (*keep out*), 芝生に入るべからず (*keep off the grass*), 座るべからず (*do not sit*).\n\nConnection: V-る + べからず, like べき. する becomes **するべからず** or the more classical **すべからず**.\n\nForms:\n- **〜べからず** ends a sentence: a rule, a sign.\n- **〜べからざる + N** modifies a noun (the book's ＊ note): 許すべからざる行為 (*an unforgivable act*), 欠くべからざる条件 (*an indispensable condition*). In modern prose this is the form you meet most.\n- Its positive partner is **〜べし** (*shall, must*), as in the sample text's 筆順を守るべし (*follow the stroke order*).\n\nRegister: nobody says 座るべからず to a friend (you'd say 座らないで or 座っちゃだめ). In writing it evokes a solemn old rule, sometimes playfully: the sample lists the traditional rules of calligraphy, the reading passage opens with 嘘をつくべからず (*thou shalt not lie*), and the author in the listening sums up his book as 他人任せにして安心するべからず (*don't leave it to others and rest easy*).\n\nCompare:\n- **〜べきではない**: *should not*, an opinion or advice: 嘘をつくべきではない (*you shouldn't lie*). It argues; べからず commands.\n- **〜てはならない／〜てはいけない**: ordinary prohibitions.\n- **〜べく** (#90): *in order to*: 勝つべく練習する (*practice in order to win*).\n- **〜べくもない** (#122): *there's no way to*: 知るべくもない (*there was no way of knowing*).\n- **VべくしてVた** (#52): *was bound to*: 起こるべくして起こった (*it was bound to happen*).\n- Don't confuse it with the adverb **すべからく** (*as a matter of course, by all means*).\n\nCommon mistakes: ✗許すべからず行為 → ✓許すべからざる行為 (a noun follows). ✗座ったべからず → ✓座るべからず (dictionary form only).\n\nJLPT tip: sentence-final → べからず; before a noun → べからざる. Check that the context is a rule or notice: 危険につき、この橋渡るべからず (*Danger: do not cross this bridge*). In 問題1, べからざる is a distractor wherever no noun follows the blank.",
