          deepDive: "**のみ** is the written, formal counterpart of **だけ** (*only*). Putting the adverb **ただ** in front (ただ〜のみ) doubles the limitation: *nothing but ~, solely ~*. The book defines it as 本当に〜だけだ: ただ自分の心を表現することのみを目指す (*they aim solely to express their own hearts*).\n\nConnection:\n- **N + のみ**: 風の音のみ (*only the sound of the wind*), 強さのみ (*strength alone*), 弟のテオのみ (*only his brother Theo*). Case particles can follow: のみが, のみを, のみで, のみに.\n- **V-る + のみ(だ)** at the end of a sentence: *all that is left / all one can do is ~*: あとはただ結果を待つのみだ (*all that is left is to wait for the result*). It expresses resolve or the single remaining step.\n- **〜のみとなる**: *only ~ remains*: 1カ月を残すのみとなりました (*only one month remains*).\n\nRegister: だけ is neutral and conversational; のみ is stiff and bookish (news, notices such as 会員のみ入場可 *members only*, essays, speeches). In speech it sounds solemn or dramatic, which can be the point, as in the coach's 強さのみが王者の条件ではない (*strength alone doesn't make a champion*).\n\nLogic: のみ excludes everything else, and the rest of the sentence must fit that. ✓ただ1人のみで、他の人々は間に合わなかった (*only one made it; the others were too late*) / ✗…、その1人は間に合わなかった. 〜を待つのみ presupposes that everything else is done: ✓準備が完了して、あとは待つのみ / ✗準備が始まって、あとは待つのみ.\n\nCompare:\n- **〜しか〜ない**: *only* with a negative verb: 1人しか来なかった (*only one person came*). Don't mix them up: 1人のみ来なかった means *only one person didn't come*.\n- **〜ばかり**: *nothing but*, often disapproving: 遊んでばかりいる (*does nothing but play*).\n- **〜のみならず**: *not only ~ (but also)*: 国内のみならず海外でも (*not only at home but abroad too*).\n- **〜をおいて他にない** (#40): *no one/nothing but ~*, with high praise: 彼をおいて他にいない (*there's no one but him*). のみ simply limits; をおいて evaluates.\n\nJLPT tip: look for ただ or a context of exclusion (あとは〜のみ, 〜のみとなる). In 問題1, のみ also appears as a distractor: it cannot fill a conditional like あいつ（　）いなければ (*if only that guy weren't around*), which needs さえ.",
